{"decision":"PROPOSE","invalid_operation":null,"observations":["The source problem asks for the smallest positive integer a for which an integer-coefficient polynomial P can take value a at odd points 1,3,5,7 and -a at even points 2,4,6,8.","The failed attempt derived necessary divisibility conditions (a divisible by 3,5,7) and concluded a=105, but did not rigorously verify sufficiency or account for all modular obstructions.","A general mechanism is to model the existence of an integer-coefficient polynomial with prescribed values at finitely many integer points via congruence conditions on finite differences, or equivalently via the integer-valued polynomial / binomial basis.","For a polynomial with integer coefficients, the divided differences at integer nodes must satisfy strong integrality constraints; the minimal positive scaling a can be found by computing the gcd of certain integer combinations of the prescribed values.","The problem can be parameterized by two disjoint finite sets of integer nodes (odd and even positions) and a sign pattern, then solved by checking whether the unique interpolation polynomial scaled by a has integer coefficients for the minimal a satisfying all congruences."],"parameter_schema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"nodes":{"description":"Distinct integer nodes x_1,...,x_n where the polynomial values are prescribed.","items":{"type":"integer"},"type":"array"},"signs":{"description":"Integer signs s_1,...,s_n such that P(x_i)=s_i*a for the unknown positive integer a. Must have same length as nodes.","items":{"type":"integer"},"type":"array"}},"required":["nodes","signs"],"type":"object"},"procedure_plan":"Define a general computation: given a list of integer nodes x_1,...,x_n and prescribed integer values v_1,...,v_n (up to an unknown positive integer scale a, i.e., v_i = s_i * a with fixed integer signs s_i), determine the smallest positive integer a such that there exists a polynomial P in Z[x] with P(x_i)=s_i*a for all i. Algorithm: (1) Compute the unique interpolation polynomial Q(x) in Q[x] of degree < n with Q(x_i)=s_i (rational coefficients). (2) The general polynomial with integer coefficients matching the values is P(x)=a*Q(x)+M(x)*R(x) where R(x)=prod_i (x-x_i) and M in Z[x]. (3) The condition that P has integer coefficients is equivalent to: for each coefficient index k, a*[x^k]Q(x) + [x^k](M(x)R(x)) is integer. Since M can be chosen freely in Z[x], the obstruction is that a*Q(x) must be congruent modulo the ideal generated by R(x) to a polynomial with integer coefficients. (4) A practical finite check: the values P(x_i) are fixed, and integer-coefficient polynomials are characterized by the condition that all finite differences at the nodes are divisible by appropriate factorials (or more precisely, the Newton interpolation coefficients in the binomial basis must be integers). Compute the Newton divided differences of the data (s_i) at the nodes; let d_j be the j-th divided difference (a rational number). Then a*d_j must be an integer for all j (since the Newton basis polynomials binom(x-x_1, j) have denominator j! and integer coefficients only when the divided differences are integers). Actually the correct condition: write the interpolation in the Newton form P(x)=sum_j c_j * prod_{k<j}(x-x_k) with c_j rational. For P to have integer coefficients, it is necessary and sufficient that a*c_j * (product of leading coefficients) ... The cleanest finite criterion: the values at n+1 consecutive integers determine the polynomial; use the fact that a polynomial with integer coefficients satisfies that the n-th finite difference is divisible by n!. More concretely, compute the integer-valued polynomial basis: any polynomial in Z[x] can be written as sum_j b_j * binom(x, j) with b_j integers? No, that's for integer-valued polynomials, not integer-coefficient. For integer-coefficient polynomials, the condition is that the coefficients in the standard monomial basis are integers. So compute the rational interpolation polynomial Q(x) explicitly (degree < n). Then the set of a such that a*Q(x) has integer coefficients modulo the ideal (R(x)) intersected with Z[x] is a subgroup of Z; find its positive generator. This can be done by computing the Smith normal form or by checking congruences: for each prime p, determine the minimal p-adic valuation of a required. Specifically, for each prime p, consider the system: find the minimal e_p >= 0 such that there exists M in Z[x] with a*Q(x) + M(x)R(x) having all coefficients divisible by p^{e_p} when a is a multiple of p^{e_p}. This is a linear algebra problem over Z/p^e. The overall minimal a is the product over primes of p^{e_p}. Since the nodes are small (1..8), we can compute this by brute force over a reasonable range or by solving the congruence conditions via the Chinese Remainder Theorem on the coefficients. A simpler equivalent: the minimal a is the least common multiple of the denominators of the coefficients of Q(x) after clearing the content of R(x)? Not exactly, because M(x)R(x) can adjust coefficients. The correct condition: a*Q(x) must be in Z[x] + (R(x)) where (R(x)) is the ideal generated by R(x) in Z[x]. The quotient Z[x]/(R(x)) is a free Z-module of rank n with basis 1,x,...,x^{n-1}. The class of a*Q(x) in this quotient must lie in the image of Z^n under the map sending basis elements to their remainders modulo R(x). Since R(x) is monic, the remainder of any polynomial modulo R(x) is just the polynomial itself if degree < n. So the condition is simply that a*Q(x) has integer coefficients! Because any polynomial of degree < n with integer coefficients is already in Z[x]. Wait: P(x) = a*Q(x) + M(x)R(x). If a*Q(x) has integer coefficients, then P has integer coefficients (take M=0). Conversely, if P has integer coefficients, then a*Q(x) = P(x) - M(x)R(x) has integer coefficients (since R is monic with integer coefficients, M in Z[x] implies M*R in Z[x]). So the condition is exactly that a*Q(x) has integer coefficients. Therefore the minimal a is the least common multiple of the denominators of the coefficients of Q(x) (when Q is written in lowest terms). This is a clean, finite computation. So the algorithm: (1) Given nodes x_i and signs s_i, compute the unique polynomial Q(x) in Q[x] of degree < n with Q(x_i)=s_i. (2) Compute the least common multiple L of the denominators of the coefficients of Q(x) (in reduced form). (3) The minimal positive a is L. (4) Verify by checking that L*Q(x) has integer coefficients and that no smaller positive integer works (by definition of LCM of denominators). This gives the exact answer. For the source problem, nodes are 1..8, signs +1 for odd, -1 for even. Compute Q(x) via Lagrange interpolation or solving the Vandermonde system over Q. Then compute LCM of denominators. This should yield the correct minimal a. The failed attempt got 105, but the correct answer is likely different (perhaps 315 or 105? We need to compute). The mechanism is sound and generalizes to any finite set of integer nodes and integer target values up to scale.","replacement_operation":null,"source_parameters":{"nodes":[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8],"signs":[1,-1,1,-1,1,-1,1,-1]},"uncertainty":"The main uncertainty is whether the minimal a is indeed the LCM of denominators of the interpolation polynomial Q(x) with Q(x_i)=s_i. This relies on the fact that any integer-coefficient polynomial matching the values must be of the form a*Q(x)+M(x)R(x) with M in Z[x], and that a*Q(x) having integer coefficients is necessary and sufficient. This is correct because R(x) is monic. However, one must be careful: Q(x) is the unique polynomial of degree < n, but P could have degree >= n. The representation P = a*Q + M*R holds for any polynomial P with the given values, where M is a polynomial with rational coefficients; but if P has integer coefficients, then M = (P - a*Q)/R must have integer coefficients because R is monic and P - a*Q has integer coefficients (since a*Q may not, but P does, so a*Q = P - M*R; if M has integer coefficients then a*Q has integer coefficients; conversely if a*Q has integer coefficients then M=0 works). So the condition is exactly that a*Q has integer coefficients. Thus the minimal a is the LCM of denominators of Q's coefficients. This is a clean theorem. The only risk is computational error in computing Q(x) for the specific nodes. The plan is implementable and deterministic."}